ified as an unrelated or abandoned mutation because it served a clear rollback purpose.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3ce47e4fb0584be8","cmd-cf796860362b4693","cmd-1667e58bf4624555","cmd-53c49bbf88b04284","cmd-8027d0f95d58475e","cmd-01800d2a2bda4823","cmd-2fa82a13d3524341","cmd-8f6944de263641b6","cmd-8ba12d72526142f6","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The task is partially satisfied: node1 demonstrably locks a local account on the fifth failed password authentication and not after four, with configuration and enforcement surviving a reboot. The configured timeout is 300 seconds, but five-minute automatic expiry was not behaviorally demonstrated. The mandatory restart protocol is otherwise complete for the sole managed node: healthy pre-state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, recovered sshd/system state, retained PAM configuration, and a successful post-restart functional retest.","limitations":["No bounded test demonstrated continued denial during the five-minute interval followed by automatic successful authentication after expiry.","Lifecycle package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency was not independently observed; the command trace contains no package-changing action.","The rollback backup created under /root was not included in final cleanup or inventory evidence, so its final presence cannot be directly confirmed.","The after-prepare snapshot aliases before-prepare because preparation was disabled; its boot-identity cross-check is therefore represented by global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity and compared with global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity."]}
